TOUCH(1)                      Funny CLI Manual                      TOUCH(1)

NAME
    touch — Create a file if it doesn't exist, or politely remind an
            existing file that you still know where it lives.

SYNOPSIS
    touch <file>

DESCRIPTION
    The 'touch' command creates the specified file if it does not
    already exist.

    If the filename has no extension, '.txt' is automatically added.

    So:

        touch notes

    becomes:

        touch notes.txt

    If the file already exists, 'touch' leaves it alone and reports
    that it has been touched.

    No files are harmed during this process.

ARGUMENTS
    file
        The name or path of the file to touch.

        Required. If omitted, the command refuses to touch anything
        on principle.

FILE EXTENSIONS
    If the supplied name contains no '.', the command automatically
    appends '.txt'.

        touch notes
            → notes.txt

        touch notes.md
            → notes.md

        touch archive.tar.gz
            → archive.tar.gz

    Because apparently this command has opinions about filenames.

OUTPUT
    On success:

        Touched <path>

    Whether the file was created or already existed, the command
    considers the mission accomplished.

ERRORS
    touch error: <error>
        Something prevented the file from being touched.

        Possible causes include:
        - Permission denied.
        - The parent directory doesn't exist.
        - The path is invalid.
        - The filesystem has declared the file emotionally unavailable.

USAGE ERRORS
    > touch

        Usage: touch <file>

        You forgot the file.

        You cannot touch what you cannot name.

EXAMPLES
    > touch notes
        Touched /some/path/notes.txt

        You asked for 'notes'. You got 'notes.txt'.

    > touch README.md
        Touched /some/path/README.md

        A perfectly respectable extension.

    > touch todo.txt
        Touched /some/path/todo.txt

        The to-do list now exists.

        Unfortunately, the tasks probably still do too.

    > touch existing.txt
        Touched /some/path/existing.txt

        It was already there.

        But now it has been acknowledged.

    > touch very.important
        Touched /some/path/very.important

        The presence of a dot has convinced the command that this
        is already an appropriately named file.

FUN FACTS
    - Missing extensions automatically become '.txt'.
    - Existing files are allowed because 'exist_ok=True' said so.
    - The command does not complain if the file already exists.
    - A filename containing any '.' is considered to have an extension,
      even if the dot is technically just part of the name.
    - Despite its name, no physical touching occurs.
    - Your computer remains disappointingly intangible.

SEE ALSO
